WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.860
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.790
So we continue our discussion
on the Feynman Calculus,

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:14.750
and continue now talking
about Fermi's Golden Rule.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:18.050
The heart of calculating
decay rates and cross sections

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.520
is Fermi's Golden Rule.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.650
And it simply tells
you how we can

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.770
use the calculation
of amplitudes

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:30.200
and the available phase space to
make assessment of decay rates

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.800
and cross sections.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.560
So the amplitude M holds all
the dynamical information.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.550
And we can see that we can
calculate the amplitude

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.130
by evaluating Feynman diagrams
directly using Feynman rules.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.790
The available phase space
holds is a kinematic factor,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.640
and it depends on
masses and the energies

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.590
and the momentum of
the particles involved.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.380
And then again, Fermi's
Golden Rule simply

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.790
says that the transition rate or
decay rates and cross sections

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.490
are given by the product
of the phase space

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.950
and the square of the amplitude.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.750
How does this now look like?

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.970
If you look at the
Golden Rule for Decays,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.800
here we suppose
having one particle

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.970
decaying into a second,
third, fourth, n-th particle.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.590
This is that the
decay rate is given

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:28.060
by matrix element, the amplitude
squared, and a term which

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.420
is C, the phase space factor.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.800
There's also a
factor here in front.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.700
This S has to count-- we have
to account for the fact that we

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.880
might have the same
particle in the final state,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.040
or the same particle
occurring multiple times.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.540
And we have to make sure that
we don't have double counting.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.910
In this double counting
has to be correct.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:53.360
If all particles are different,
this extra factor is 1.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.900
We'll look at this
some more later.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.570
Now, at first glance, this
looks like rather complicated.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.700
But if you try to
assess now what

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.340
those individual
terms mean, you will

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.020
see that it's very accessible.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:14.160
So when we try to calculate
a use from this golden rule

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.480
for the case, we to integrate
over all outgoing particle

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.650
four-momenta.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.070
But we have three
kinematical constraints.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.580
The first one is that
the outgoing particles

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.420
have to be on mass.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.450
So they have to
be on mass shell.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:36.090
We talked about this issue of
virtual particle [? inertia ?]

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:36.930
before.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.040
But simply, they have to--

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.610
the energy of the particle
has to follow this condition.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.380
This is a delta
function, which simply

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.820
means that this gives
us, if the argument is

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.730
0, the delta
function, which was 1.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.530
The argument is non-zero.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.000
The function returns 0.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.700
So this [INAUDIBLE]
simplifies this term here.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:05.490
So this first part here in
our Fermi's Golden Rule simply

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.690
accounts for the fact
that outgoing particles

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.750
have to be on mass shell.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.590
Outgoing particles also have
to have positive energies.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.140
And this explains our second
factor here, this factor.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.910
And so this factor is
the heavy side function,

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.940
and this is simply 0
for negative values

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.290
and 1 for positive values.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.240
And the last one means
that energy and momentum

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.900
of the particle
have to conserve.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.330
So the first particle
minus the second,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.370
third, and so on for each
of the component for energy

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.700
and for those [? three ?]
components of the momenta

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.330
have to be 0 for
this to return 1.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.270
So again, another
delta function.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.550
That's also factors of pi.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.980
And the simple rule here
is for each delta function,

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.800
you have to account for a
vector of 2 pi in your function.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.160
So this basically
explains already

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.170
everything we see
here on this slide.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.140
So now we can calculate.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.320
And I recommend to have a
look at Griffiths chapter

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.820
6 for this.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.110
If you look at two
particle decays.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.980
So one particle
into two particles.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.050
This simplifies
quite tremendously

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.620
because of all the
delta functions here

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.150
and the heavy side functions.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.280
This equation simplifies
directly to a factor.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.900
You have the momentum
of the particle

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.780
here, and a matrix element.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.820
Again you have this
statistical factor here

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.850
to account for the fact that
there might be duplicates

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.980
and you want to keep track
of the statistical factor.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.230
For scattering, the equation
looks almost the same.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:51.570
So you have the same
phase space factor.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.765
Almost the same
phase space factor.

00:04:53.765 --> 00:04:54.660
Matrix element.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.110
Again, this is
the transition way

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.170
it is given by, as Fermi's
Golden Rule tells us,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:05.100
by the matrix elements squared.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.230
And the phase space factor.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.250
This overall effect,
as we look at later,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.360
they are slightly different.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.840
But here, as a rule,
we want to make sure

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.100
that we have a way to
assess cross section.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.640
Did this make sense?

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.100
We'll see later in more detail.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.530
For two body decays in
the center of mass frame.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.180
You know that the
initial momenta

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.420
in the center of mass frames
of particle 1 and particle 2

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.840
have to be the same.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.360
The outgoing momenta
also have to be the same.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.620
They don't necessarily have to
be the same as the [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.760
But if any uses of the
differential cross-section

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.750
can be calculated quite
straightforwardly.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.180
Again, there's a
matrix element squared.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.270
You have the final state
momenta, the initial state

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.200
momenta.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.990
And divide this by the sum of
the energies squared together

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.020
with an extra factor here.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.410
So what we have seen here is
just we have looked at it.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.500
I didn't explain
how we got to this.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.150
But we have seen
from this Golden

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.370
Rule, which helps us to assess.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.950
And we have seen how we can
calculate the phase space

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:18.630
factor.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:22.560
The next lecture, we now
see how we can calculate

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.180
the matrix element itself.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.080
And we'll start doing this by
using a toy experiment or toy

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.220
model, such that the discussion
simplifies, algebra simplifies

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.040
quite a bit.

